From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_STREAM=1: the shader-set extractor no longer cuts zstd content silently at its 256 MiB cap; BNSH data over the cap is cut in streamed passes (Ocarina's Model.Product.110 bfsha.zs: the 11,788 missing programs); EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_STREAM_VERIFY=1 cross-checks; EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_INDEX=1 reads only the files nxnative's shader map names; all off by default

Not a Fritchii port: a fix to our 0501 extractor, using data from nxnative
(github.com/seedds/nxnative 47773ed1d2, MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 seedds, dm/gfx shader_map.tsv).
Fritchii v5.6 has no EXP number for this; the switch names are ours.

Why 29,381 of 41,216: nxnative's map joins 41,169 of the recipe's fingerprints to their RomFS
source. 11,788 come from /Shader/Model.Product.110.product.Nin_Ounce_NVN2.bfsha.zs, and
41,169 - 11,788 = 29,381 is our "found" count exactly (11,788 + 47 unmapped = 11,835 "not found").
That file holds two BNSH, at 0xf7d000 and 0x11543000 (277 MiB into the inflated content). 0501's
Inflate(..., MaxBlob) stops at exactly 256 MiB with no error (it throws only when limit > MaxBlob),
so EmbeddedBnsh saw the first BNSH run past the end (not bounded, skipped) and never saw the second.
The "over the cap" error in File() compares the compressed size, so nothing was logged.

EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_STREAM=1 (Top() and Walk()'s zstd branch go through InflateMember):
- Content under the cap: inflated and walked exactly as before.
- Content over the cap (declared in the frame header, or found when Inflate stops at the cap with
  the frame unfinished: one more byte is asked for): counted, then
  - BNSH data (content Walk() would only search for BNSH: not zstd/Yaz0/SARC/BAHS/NDSR/NDVE) is
    cut in passes over the compressed bytes already in memory: (1) BNSH magic positions and the
    content size, (2) each hit's first 4 KiB, (3..6) the reads a line-for-line copy of Bnsh()
    still misses (variation table, program records, stage records, control sections), (7) every
    planned program's code, held only while it streams by and handed to Emit() with its control
    section. EmbeddedBnsh's bounds, skip rule, error text and origin labels are kept, so the
    found programs are the ones a whole-buffer Bnsh() would cut. About 7 inflate passes of
    ~300 MiB for this file; peak memory is the kept tables and control sections plus a 4 MiB
    window, instead of today's 256 MiB buffer for this file.
  - Anything else over the cap is an archive error ("is a container, not BNSH data").
- Programs from a streamed member come in code order, not variation order. The set keys
  programs by fingerprint and uses neither origin nor variations.
- The stamp gains "extractor stream 1".

EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_STREAM_VERIFY=1 (needs _STREAM=1): every BNSH member under the cap is walked
as usual and also cut the streamed way; the two lists (stage, fingerprint, origin, as multisets)
must match, else an archive error "stream verify: ..." (the set is then not stamped).
One extra log line counts members over the cap, streamed, passes and verified.

EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_INDEX=1: reads shader_set_index.txt beside the .nx2recipe (one RomFS source per
line, as nxnative's map names them; prefixes up to each '/' match the container file). Only those
RomFS files are read (OoT: 1,140 sources, against 186,587 files, 23.5 GB, 826 s today); the zstd
dictionaries are always read. A missing or empty index logs a warning and reads every file. The
stamp gains "extractor index 1". The data file is claude-out/shader_set_index.oot.txt (with
nxnative's MIT notice); it is not part of this patch.

With all three unset: Inflate's new out-parameter is null, Top() and Walk() take the old branch,
Extract() gets no index, the Stats fields stay 0, no log line is added and StampText() appends ""
(byte for byte the old stamp).

Applies after 0501 + 0503 (no later patch touches these files). Not compiled, not run.

v2 (review): StreamOn/StreamVerify are read at first use, not at namespace scope; a
namespace-scope value is set before main applies env.txt, so the switch could never turn on
from env.txt while the stamp still said "extractor stream 1".

